WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.475
A useful schedule explains why work begins when it does.

00:00:04.595 --> 00:00:11.945
In this lesson we will connect tasks, compare dependency
relations, add a lag, and adjust working calendars.

00:00:12.065 --> 00:00:16.990
We will also distinguish authored timeline scale from
preview magnification.

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:22.435
The customer portal plan gives us a small example that is
easy to inspect.

00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:30.680
Keep checking the source as you change the rules: an
arrow's appearance is less important than the start or end
condition it represents.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.350
Start with independently dated tasks.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:44.620
Use Add dependency to choose a predecessor and successor,
then choose which endpoint of each task participates.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:48.265
We will connect Research's end to Design's start.

00:00:48.385 --> 00:00:52.210
Check the generated statement and the resulting dates.

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:56.930
A dependency is a rule, not merely an annotation on the
drawing.

00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:06.500
If you connect the wrong pair, inspect or remove the link
rather than trying to compensate by dragging unrelated
tasks into a convenient-looking arrangement.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.350
A start can follow another task's end or start, and an end
can follow another task's end or start.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.945
These four relations express different constraints.

00:01:20.065 --> 00:01:26.565
We will compare valid source examples, checking the
relationship shown by the inspector after each one.

00:01:26.685 --> 00:01:32.635
The end-constrained examples keep an explicit start so the
duration has a clear basis.

00:01:32.755 --> 00:01:37.805
Return to the original end-to-start rule after comparing
the endpoints.

00:01:37.925 --> 00:01:41.900
Do not choose a relation just because its arrow looks
tidy.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:48.120
State which event constrains the work and verify that the
resulting dates express that requirement.

00:01:51.633 --> 00:01:54.608
A dependency can include an offset.

00:01:54.728 --> 00:02:00.303
Open the Research-to-Design link and set a lag of two days
after the predecessor's end.

00:02:00.423 --> 00:02:06.923
For the statement N days after A's end, the candidate
start is A's last day, plus one, plus N.

00:02:07.043 --> 00:02:12.993
If that candidate is closed in the working calendar, the
start moves to the next working day.

00:02:13.113 --> 00:02:18.538
This is different from treating the predecessor's last day
as the successor's first day.

00:02:18.658 --> 00:02:24.358
Read the authored expression and calculated date together,
especially across a weekend.

00:02:27.867 --> 00:02:34.092
The zero-lag start-at-end rule places Design on Friday,
the sixth, in this working calendar.

00:02:34.212 --> 00:02:38.387
In the source, change the rule to one day after Research
ends.

00:02:38.507 --> 00:02:45.332
The candidate date falls on a closed Saturday, so the
working calendar advances Design to Monday the ninth.

00:02:45.452 --> 00:02:51.452
Hover over Design to read its resulting dates and compare
them with the explicit source rule.

00:02:51.572 --> 00:02:57.422
Closed-day advancement must follow the same working
calendar used to interpret the schedule.

00:03:00.933 --> 00:03:06.733
The dependency inspector lets you change the endpoint
tasks as well as the relation.

00:03:06.853 --> 00:03:13.303
In a practice copy, redirect the predecessor from Research
to Build and inspect what the app permits.

00:03:13.423 --> 00:03:18.723
Avoid creating a cycle: a task cannot ultimately depend on
itself.

00:03:18.843 --> 00:03:26.193
Line style, color and an optional note communicate the
dependency's meaning, but they do not replace the
scheduling rule.

00:03:26.313 --> 00:03:33.763
Return to the intended pair or delete an unwanted link,
then use Undo to reconsider the change if necessary.

00:03:37.267 --> 00:03:42.892
In this fixed-task example, choose Only task and retain
Build’s authored start date.

00:03:43.012 --> 00:03:47.287
Move Design by one day and verify that Build keeps its
start.

00:03:47.407 --> 00:03:51.132
Undo that edit before comparing the other policy.

00:03:51.252 --> 00:03:57.277
Choose Include dependents and move Design again; the
connected Build task now moves with it.

00:03:57.397 --> 00:04:02.422
One shared Undo restores the complete cascade as a single
source change.

00:04:02.542 --> 00:04:09.992
Relative start statements express their own scheduling
rules, so check the authored rules as well as the movement
preference.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:19.400
Use separate task declarations and scheduling statements
to make this rule easy to review.

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:25.670
Replace Design’s fixed start statement with a start at
Research’s end, and check the current preview.

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:30.165
The relative rule records why the start follows the
earlier task.

00:04:30.285 --> 00:04:36.910
Replace it with the original fixed date and verify that
the source and preview return to the starting state.

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:42.005
Hover over Design to read the resulting task date after
the source edits.

00:04:42.125 --> 00:04:46.525
Keep property inspectors closed during these rule
replacements.

00:04:50.033 --> 00:04:55.533
Select the Design to Build dependency and inspect its
predecessor and successor.

00:04:55.653 --> 00:05:02.728
Change the predecessor to the independent Review task,
then check the generated reference and the current
schedule.

00:05:02.848 --> 00:05:09.648
Reconnecting an endpoint changes which work constrains
Build; it is not just moving an arrow for appearance.

00:05:09.768 --> 00:05:16.093
Delete that link in the disposable copy and verify that
the old relation is no longer authored.

00:05:16.213 --> 00:05:22.338
Inspect dates after removing a constraint so the resulting
schedule remains understandable.

00:05:25.867 --> 00:05:32.667
In this disposable example, try making Design depend on
Build while Build already depends on Design.

00:05:32.787 --> 00:05:37.312
That request creates a cycle rather than a meaningful
order of work.

00:05:37.432 --> 00:05:42.457
Inspect the validation feedback and the authored result
before continuing.

00:05:42.577 --> 00:05:47.877
Do not try to repair a cycle simply by dragging bars until
they look separated.

00:05:47.997 --> 00:05:55.772
Restore the known good source and verify the current
preview, then choose dependencies that express a
consistent direction of work.

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:01.800
Open Calendar and schedule.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:05.945
The project start and closed weekdays are authored
settings.

00:06:06.065 --> 00:06:12.790
This example closes Saturday and Sunday, so five working
days can span a longer calendar interval.

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:16.785
Compare the weekday controls with the source declarations.

00:06:16.905 --> 00:06:21.930
Changing the calendar changes the schedule calculation,
not just the picture.

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:32.900
Use a calendar that reflects the work being planned, and
explain exceptions rather than silently changing every
task's duration to compensate for holidays or non-working
periods.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:41.525
Use Add exception to mark a specific day or range as
closed or opened.

00:06:41.645 --> 00:06:44.345
We will close the tenth of November.

00:06:44.465 --> 00:06:49.890
Set the From and To dates to that day and leave the state
closed, then choose Apply.

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:53.410
Inspect the source and the affected task dates.

00:06:53.530 --> 00:07:00.330
An opened exception can represent a special working day,
while a closed exception can represent a holiday.

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:06.875
These are changes to the shared plan, so include the
assumptions when reviewing the schedule with other people.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:16.500
Sometimes the whole project is working, but a particular
task cannot proceed on a certain day.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:20.945
A task pause is different from closing that day for every
task.

00:07:21.065 --> 00:07:28.315
Inspect Design's pause controls and compare a task-
specific pause with the calendar exception we just
created.

00:07:28.435 --> 00:07:32.485
Use the narrowest rule that expresses the real constraint.

00:07:32.605 --> 00:07:41.230
After applying a pause, check the effective end date and
any dependent work; a correct local change can still move
the overall delivery.

00:07:44.733 --> 00:07:51.608
The Timeline scale control changes the authored time axis:
daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly or yearly.

00:07:51.728 --> 00:07:55.203
Preview zoom only magnifies the current drawing.

00:07:55.323 --> 00:07:59.048
Choose weekly scale and apply it, then compare the source.

00:07:59.168 --> 00:08:03.943
Use this distinction when preparing a long-range view for
stakeholders.

00:08:04.063 --> 00:08:10.063
A monthly overview may be useful for a programme, while
daily detail is useful for short work.

00:08:10.183 --> 00:08:14.933
Neither choice changes the task effort simply to make the
diagram look smaller.

00:08:18.467 --> 00:08:23.817
Use calendar and display settings for highlights, title,
header, footer and caption.

00:08:23.937 --> 00:08:31.637
Highlighting a deadline makes it easier to discuss, but it
does not create a dependency or promise that the date is
achievable.

00:08:31.757 --> 00:08:36.832
Diagram filters can hide work from the canvas while
leaving the source intact.

00:08:36.952 --> 00:08:40.352
Legend labels explain colors for the reader.

00:08:40.472 --> 00:08:49.672
Keep these presentation choices consistent with the
scheduling facts, and avoid using color alone as the only
way to convey a critical distinction.

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:56.925
The blue Research task supplies a color for the legend.

00:08:57.045 --> 00:09:01.245
Open Legend labels and name that color Discovery work.

00:09:01.365 --> 00:09:06.290
Apply the label, then reopen the dialog to verify that it
was retained.

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:12.160
A legend explains the visual meaning of a color; it does
not change a task dependency.

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:18.305
Canvas filters and report scope remain separate choices,
as shown in the reporting lesson.

00:09:21.833 --> 00:09:26.433
Pause and create an end-to-start dependency with a two-day
lag.

00:09:26.553 --> 00:09:34.303
State the predecessor's last day, calculate the candidate
start using the plus-one rule, and then account for closed
days.

00:09:34.423 --> 00:09:38.648
Add the November holiday and compare the calculated
result.

00:09:38.768 --> 00:09:44.143
Switch to weekly timeline scale, then use preview zoom
without editing the source.

00:09:44.263 --> 00:09:51.838
Your answer should explain each movement with a rule,
rather than simply report that one bar became longer or
moved to the right.

00:09:55.367 --> 00:10:02.142
You can now create and inspect dependencies, distinguish
their endpoints, and account for lag and closed days.

00:10:02.262 --> 00:10:11.762
You can express project-wide calendar exceptions
separately from task pauses, and choose a useful timeline
scale without confusing it with magnification.

00:10:11.882 --> 00:10:16.357
Keep the source and calculated dates together when
reviewing a change.

00:10:16.477 --> 00:10:23.827
The next lesson adds people and capacity, because a plan
also needs to explain who is available to perform the
work.
